WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.871
Most divide-and-conquer algorithms

00:00:02.946 --> 00:00:05.290
have a recurrence of one shape.

00:00:05.366 --> 00:00:07.786
Split a problem of size n into a

00:00:07.861 --> 00:00:10.054
subproblems of size n/b

00:00:10.054 --> 00:00:11.869
solve them recursively

00:00:11.944 --> 00:00:14.062
and spend O(n^d) on

00:00:14.137 --> 00:00:15.876
dividing and combining.

00:00:15.952 --> 00:00:17.842
The master theorem solves

00:00:17.918 --> 00:00:19.430
all of them at once

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:22.077
T(n) is at most aT(n/b)

00:00:22.077 --> 00:00:23.740
plus O(n^d).

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.310
If a = b^d

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.494
T(n) is O(n^d log n).

00:00:29.494 --> 00:00:32.390
If a < b^d

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:34.609
it is O(n^d).

00:00:34.609 --> 00:00:38.180
And if a > b^d

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:42.330
it is O(n^(log_b a)).

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:46.914
a is at least 1, b is greater than 1

00:00:46.914 --> 00:00:49.393
and d is at least 0.

00:00:49.496 --> 00:00:52.388
Rounding n/b up or down

00:00:52.491 --> 00:00:55.280
does not change the answer.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.216
What do the three letters mean?

00:00:58.216 --> 00:01:00.268
a is the branching factor

00:01:00.268 --> 00:01:03.460
how many recursive calls each call makes.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.180
b is the shrinking factor

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.370
each subproblem has size n/b.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.564
And d describes the work outside

00:01:12.653 --> 00:01:15.942
the recursive calls: O(n^d)

00:01:16.031 --> 00:01:18.609
to create the subproblems and

00:01:18.698 --> 00:01:20.920
to combine their answers.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.577
So the three cases are a race

00:01:23.657 --> 00:01:25.169
between two speeds.

00:01:25.248 --> 00:01:28.510
a says how fast the number of subproblems

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.215
grows from one level to the next

00:01:31.295 --> 00:01:33.920
b^d says how fast the work

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.830
per subproblem shrinks.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:37.883
Six examples.

00:01:37.883 --> 00:01:39.582
Binary search

00:01:39.582 --> 00:01:44.454
T(n) is at most T(n/2) + c.

00:01:44.454 --> 00:01:48.306
So a is 1, b is 2, d is 0

00:01:48.306 --> 00:01:52.272
and b^d is 1, equal to a.

00:01:52.272 --> 00:01:56.351
Case 1: O(n^0 log n)

00:01:56.351 --> 00:01:58.730
which is O(log n).

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:03.177
T(n/2) + c_1 n: a is 1

00:02:03.177 --> 00:02:05.909
less than 2^1.

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:07.800
Case 2, O(n).

00:02:07.905 --> 00:02:10.742
Cutting the problem in half

00:02:10.847 --> 00:02:13.159
but doing linear work

00:02:13.159 --> 00:02:15.680
the top call dominates.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:20.276
2T(n/2) + n^(1/2)

00:02:20.276 --> 00:02:24.233
2 is more than the square root of 2

00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:26.453
so case 3, O(n).

00:02:26.453 --> 00:02:29.830
2T(n/4) + sqrt(n)

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:33.787
4^(1/2) is 2, equal to a

00:02:33.787 --> 00:02:35.910
so O(sqrt(n) log n).

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:40.668
maxInArray2: a=2, b=2, d=0.

00:02:40.668 --> 00:02:45.415
2 is more than 1: case 3, O(n)

00:02:45.415 --> 00:02:48.353
as we found by unrolling.

00:02:48.353 --> 00:02:52.196
Merge sort, and maximum subarray

00:02:52.196 --> 00:02:55.586
2T(n/2) + cn.

00:02:55.586 --> 00:03:00.220
2 equals 2^1: O(n log n).

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.962
Every time, the same three steps

00:03:03.962 --> 00:03:06.121
read off a, b and d

00:03:06.121 --> 00:03:08.897
compare a with b^d

00:03:08.897 --> 00:03:10.850
and pick the case.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:12.563
Your turn.

00:03:12.563 --> 00:03:17.208
T(n) is at most 8T(n/2)

00:03:17.208 --> 00:03:19.700
plus c_1 n^2.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:24.798
And T(n) is at most 2T(n/8)

00:03:24.798 --> 00:03:28.310
plus c_1 n^(1/3).

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:31.297
Solve both with the master theorem.

00:03:31.297 --> 00:03:33.180
Pause and work them out.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:41.160
The first: a=8, b=2, d=2.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:45.141
2^2 is 4, less than 8

00:03:45.141 --> 00:03:46.398
case 3.

00:03:46.398 --> 00:03:49.751
O(n^(log_2 8))

00:03:49.751 --> 00:03:52.160
which is O(n^3).

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:56.065
The second: a=2, b=8

00:03:56.065 --> 00:03:57.788
d is 1/3.

00:03:57.788 --> 00:04:02.420
The cube root of 8 is 2, equal to a

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:03.497
case 1.

00:04:03.497 --> 00:04:07.590
O(n^(1/3) * log n).

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.006
Why does it work?

00:04:10.114 --> 00:04:13.461
Draw the recursion tree of T(n)

00:04:13.569 --> 00:04:18.319
= aT(n/b) + c n^d

00:04:18.319 --> 00:04:19.939
here a is 2.

00:04:19.939 --> 00:04:23.718
The root is one problem of size n

00:04:23.718 --> 00:04:25.877
doing c n^d.

00:04:25.877 --> 00:04:30.520
Level 1 has a problems of size n/b.

00:04:30.628 --> 00:04:34.083
Level 2 has a^2 problems

00:04:34.191 --> 00:04:36.890
of size n/b^2.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:40.989
At the bottom, after log_b n levels

00:04:40.989 --> 00:04:43.375
the problems have size 1

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.675
and there are a^(log_b n)

00:04:45.761 --> 00:04:47.465
of them

00:04:47.465 --> 00:04:49.680
each doing constant work.

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:53.955
So level t has a^t problems

00:04:53.955 --> 00:04:57.543
each of size n/b^t

00:04:57.543 --> 00:05:02.029
each costing c times

00:05:02.029 --> 00:05:03.150
(n/b^t)^d.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:04.501
Multiply:

00:05:04.501 --> 00:05:08.618
the work at level t is c n^d

00:05:08.618 --> 00:05:13.180
times (a / b^d)^t.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.055
Add up all the levels

00:05:16.055 --> 00:05:19.393
T(n) is at most c n^d

00:05:19.393 --> 00:05:23.148
times the sum of (a / b^d)

00:05:23.148 --> 00:05:24.191
to the t

00:05:24.191 --> 00:05:27.947
for t from 0 to log_b n.

00:05:28.051 --> 00:05:31.911
That is a geometric series with ratio

00:05:32.015 --> 00:05:35.040
r equal to a / b^d.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.908
And a geometric series behaves

00:05:37.992 --> 00:05:39.754
in one of three ways.

00:05:39.754 --> 00:05:41.936
If r is greater than 1

00:05:41.936 --> 00:05:44.034
the last term dominates.

00:05:44.034 --> 00:05:45.964
If r is less than 1

00:05:45.964 --> 00:05:49.405
the sum is at most 1 / (1 - r)

00:05:49.405 --> 00:05:50.412
a constant.

00:05:50.412 --> 00:05:53.769
If r is exactly 1, every term is 1

00:05:53.769 --> 00:05:56.790
and the sum is the number of terms.

00:05:57.140 --> 00:05:58.492
One more fact

00:05:58.588 --> 00:06:01.581
a^(log_b n) equals

00:06:01.678 --> 00:06:04.092
n^(log_b a).

00:06:04.092 --> 00:06:07.085
Take log_b of both sides

00:06:07.085 --> 00:06:11.140
and both are (log_b a) * (log_b n).

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.769
Here are the level sums as bars

00:06:14.769 --> 00:06:16.960
level 0 at the top.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.151
Case 1: a = b^d, so r is 1

00:06:21.151 --> 00:06:24.581
and every level does the same work

00:06:24.581 --> 00:06:25.914
c n^d.

00:06:25.914 --> 00:06:30.011
There are log_b n + 1 levels

00:06:30.011 --> 00:06:33.440
so T(n) is O(n^d log n).

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:37.233
Case 2: a is less than b^d

00:06:37.233 --> 00:06:39.432
so r is less than 1.

00:06:39.432 --> 00:06:43.066
The work shrinks from level to level

00:06:43.161 --> 00:06:45.552
and the sum is a constant

00:06:45.648 --> 00:06:49.568
times the first term: the root dominates.

00:06:49.568 --> 00:06:52.150
T(n) is O(n^d).

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.659
Case 3: r is greater than 1.

00:06:55.659 --> 00:06:58.540
The work grows level by level

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:02.443
and the last level dominates: the leaves.

00:07:02.443 --> 00:07:06.531
There are n^(log_b a) of them

00:07:06.531 --> 00:07:10.620
so T(n) is O(n^(log_b a)).

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:14.430
We can check the theorem numerically.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:17.747
This program computes T(n) exactly

00:07:17.747 --> 00:07:21.501
level by level, for n equal to b^k

00:07:21.501 --> 00:07:24.033
and divides it by the bound.

00:07:24.033 --> 00:07:27.350
For binary search and for merge sort

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:28.223
case 1

00:07:28.223 --> 00:07:31.628
the ratio goes 1.125

00:07:31.628 --> 00:07:33.898
1.063

00:07:33.898 --> 00:07:36.080
1.042.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:39.017
In the case 3 and case 2

00:07:39.104 --> 00:07:41.087
rows, it settles at 2.

00:07:41.087 --> 00:07:44.364
And for 3T(n/2) + n,

00:07:44.364 --> 00:07:46.779
Karatsuba's multiplication

00:07:46.779 --> 00:07:48.590
it settles at 3.

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:51.902
In every row, the ratio approaches

00:07:51.992 --> 00:07:52.979
a constant,

00:07:52.979 --> 00:07:56.300
T(n) grows exactly like the bound.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.434
The theorem needs a subproblems

00:07:59.516 --> 00:08:01.658
of the same size n/b,

00:08:01.658 --> 00:08:03.717
with b > 1.

00:08:03.717 --> 00:08:06.682
It does not solve every recurrence.

00:08:06.682 --> 00:08:09.977
Take T(n) <= aT(n-1)

00:08:09.977 --> 00:08:10.636
+ c.

00:08:10.636 --> 00:08:14.261
The size goes down by 1, not by a factor,

00:08:14.261 --> 00:08:16.320
so there is no b at all.

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:18.621
Unroll it instead:

00:08:18.621 --> 00:08:22.416
c * (1 + a + a^2)

00:08:22.416 --> 00:08:26.536
and so on, + a^(n-1)

00:08:26.536 --> 00:08:28.270
* T(1).

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:32.168
For a = 1, this is O(n),

00:08:32.168 --> 00:08:34.568
like maxInArray1.

00:08:34.568 --> 00:08:39.264
For a >= 2, it is O(a^n),

00:08:39.264 --> 00:08:40.620
exponential.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:42.981
The Towers of Hanoi

00:08:42.981 --> 00:08:46.601
T(n) = 2T(n-1)

00:08:46.601 --> 00:08:47.607
+ 1.

00:08:47.607 --> 00:08:51.730
It needs 2^n - 1 moves.

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:56.119
Second: T(n) = T(n/5)

00:08:56.119 --> 00:08:59.635
+ T(7n/10) + n.

00:08:59.635 --> 00:09:03.737
The two subproblems have different sizes,

00:09:03.737 --> 00:09:07.350
so again the theorem does not apply.

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:10.566
Draw the recursion tree anyway.

00:09:10.566 --> 00:09:12.138
The root does n.

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:15.004
Its children have sizes

00:09:15.096 --> 00:09:17.500
n/5 and 7n/10,

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:19.996
together 9/10 of n.

00:09:20.088 --> 00:09:22.584
Level 2 does at most

00:09:22.677 --> 00:09:25.820
(9/10)^2 * n, and so on.

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:29.320
The level sums shrink geometrically

00:09:29.408 --> 00:09:31.858
because 1/5 +

00:09:31.945 --> 00:09:34.045
7/10 is less than 1.

00:09:34.133 --> 00:09:36.495
So T(n) is at most n /

00:09:36.583 --> 00:09:39.820
(1 - 9/10): 10n, O(n).

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:43.758
This is the linear-time selection algorithm,

00:09:43.758 --> 00:09:45.770
the median of medians.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:47.403
To sum up.

00:09:47.403 --> 00:09:51.720
One: for T(n) <= aT(n/b)

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.037
+ O(n^d)

00:09:54.037 --> 00:09:56.880
compare a with b^d.

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:00.565
Two: equal gives n^d log n

00:10:00.565 --> 00:10:03.302
smaller gives n^d

00:10:03.302 --> 00:10:07.830
and larger gives n^(log_b a).

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:11.712
Three: in the recursion tree

00:10:11.712 --> 00:10:15.853
level t costs c * n^d

00:10:15.853 --> 00:10:20.116
times (a / b^d)^t.

00:10:20.116 --> 00:10:24.014
Recurrences with unequal sizes

00:10:24.014 --> 00:10:26.693
or that subtract one

00:10:26.693 --> 00:10:29.860
need unrolling or a tree.

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:34.511
Try these: 4T(n/2) + n

00:10:34.511 --> 00:10:38.260
+ n^2, and + n^3

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:42.450
and 9T(n/3) + 1.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:44.722
Next: maximum subarray

00:10:44.722 --> 00:10:47.813
a real divide-and-conquer algorithm

00:10:47.897 --> 00:10:51.323
where this analysis shows that the clever

00:10:51.406 --> 00:10:54.080
algorithm beats the obvious one.

